{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Since the onset of Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID19), SARS-CoV-2 infection has been circulating globally, with approximately six peak waves of outbreaks worldwide before May 26, 2023, with a cumulative number of confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infections of 76,689,575 and a total number of deaths of 6,935,889 [1], posing a serious threat to the health of populations in all countries. On May 5, 2023 [1, 2], the World Health Organization (WHO) declared that the COVID-19 epidemic no longer constituted a public health emergency of international concern. However, it is undeniable that the COVID-19 pandemic caused a large number of deaths and was devastating. The fact that SARS-CoV-2 is still mutating indicates that it remains a global health threat. The WHO study published in Nature [1, 3] also suggested that accurately tracking SARS-CoV-2 and its impact had been challenging.",
      "reason": "The introduction summarizes the global situation, ongoing mutation, and challenges in tracking SARS-CoV-2, describing current knowledge and uncertainties about the virus and its impact."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Background There is a significant increase in the number of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection reports in various countries. However, the trend of reinfection rate over time is not clear.",
      "reason": "The abstract states the unclear temporal trend in reinfection rates and frames the need for a meta-analysis to estimate time-varying reinfection rates and guide public health measures, explaining the importance of the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "The advantage of this study was that compared with the previous meta-analysis of the SARSCoV-2 reinfection rate, first, to avoid the heterogeneous impact of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection definition on SARSCoV-2 reinfection rates, so this study defined reinfection and included only literature with reinfection intervals greater than 90 days. Secondly, considering the heterogeneity of reinfection rates of different variants, we constructed a meta-regression of time-varying reinfection rates and compared it with the temporal spline regression without considering heterogeneity to explore the temporal trends of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rates, which could provide a basis for subsequent research on prediction of SARS-CoV-2 infection, and could guide significance for the future policy formulation of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection.",
      "reason": "The discussion explicitly refers to prior meta-analyses and explains how the current review differs and why it was needed (e.g., definition harmonization, meta-regression for time-varying rates), addressing the need relative to existing reviews."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "We searched PubMed, Web of Science, Medline(Ovid), Embase(Ovid), Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials, China National Knowledge Infrastructure(CNKI), Wanfang using 'COVID-19' , '2019-nCoV' , 'SARS-COV-2' , 'Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2' , 'reinfection' , 'recurrence' , 'repeat positive' and 'repeat infections' as keywords, from January 1, 2020 to March 16, 2023 (Additional file 2).",
      "reason": "The review is observational (meta-analysis of reinfection rates from cohort, case-control, and cross-sectional studies) rather than a review of interventions; therefore describing an intervention mechanism is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "A meta-regression analysis was conducted on literature reporting cases of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection globally to estimate the incidence of reinfection of SARS-CoV-2 (as of March 16, 2023), and reported according to the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and MetaAnalyses (PRISMA) guidelines (Additional file 1). This systematic review and meta-analysis was registered with PROSPERO (CRD42023411778).",
      "reason": "The review synthesizes observational epidemiological data on reinfection rates, not evaluating complex interventions or delivery contexts; therefore a logic model is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Therefore, the aim of this study was to conduct a meta-analysis to describe and summarize the current reinfection rates in different regions and among diverse populations. Furthermore, the study sought to examine trends in reinfection rates over time, so as to provide reference for the development of global unified reinfection standards and reinfection measures in the future.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly states the review aims: to conduct a meta-analysis summarizing reinfection rates across regions/populations and to examine trends in reinfection rates over time, which directly states the objectives/questions addressed."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "We searched PubMed, Web of Science, Medline(Ovid), Embase(Ovid), Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials, China National Knowledge Infrastructure(CNKI), Wanfang using 'COVID-19' , '2019-nCoV' , 'SARS-COV-2' , 'Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2' , 'reinfection' , 'recurrence' , 'repeat positive' and 'repeat infections' as keywords, from January 1, 2020 to March 16, 2023 (Additional file 2).",
      "reason": "The review investigates incidence/rates from observational studies (cohort, case-control, cross-sectional) rather than effects of interventions; therefore PICO-style planned comparisons for intervention effects do not apply."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The inclusion criteria were: (1) Conformed to research questions, i.e., studies that estimated the reinfection rate of SARS-CoV-2 or we could calculate it based on the data provided in the paper; (2) The study design included cohort study, case-control study, and descriptive study; (3) The study fited our working definition of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection. ... reinfection working definition in this study was based on a positive laboratory result at least 90 days after laboratory confirmation of primary infection(laboratory testing methods include reverse transcription-polymerase chain reaction or rapid antigen test, also called Lateral Flow Devices, and so on) ... (4) Original research. The exclusion criteria were: (1) The sample size was less than 20; (2) Belonged to one of the following types of studies: editorial, case report, case series study, systematic review, meta-analysis, animal experiment, news report; (3) Secondary reporting or articles on repeated studies of the same population; (4) Grey literature, including a range of documents not controlled by commercial publishing organizations.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly lists study-level inclusion and exclusion criteria, specifies eligible study designs (cohort, case-control, descriptive), a working reinfection definition (>=90 days), requirement for original research and minimum sample size, and exclusions (study types and grey literature)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "To avoid language bias, the search was not limited to any language. We conducted preliminary screening based on the English title and English abstract provided by nonEnglish articles. Further, in the screening process, if the included article was neither in English nor in Chinese and did not provide an English abstract or full text, we used an online translator (Google Translate) for translation. ... The exclusion criteria were: ... (4) Grey literature, including a range of documents not controlled by commercial publishing organizations.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies language handling (no language limit, translation approach) and excludes grey literature (publication status). However, it does not state criteria for dissemination year or other report-date restrictions, so the item is only partially addressed."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": "One of them reported only the reinfection rate, but not the associated number of first infections as well as the number of reinfections, and two other articles studied the same population, so the meta-analysis of single-group rates could not be performed. Therefore, we excluded the above literature with the number of unreported infections and selected one of two literature with the same population for inclusion.",
      "reason": "The review explicitly distinguishes and documents exclusion of studies because outcome numbers were not reported (reported only a rate) as distinct from other exclusions (duplicate populations), and explains how these situations were handled."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_10",
      "quote": "Subgroup analyses were performed for age, reinfection variant, country, study population, and study type respectively, and the results were shown in Table 1, and forest plots for each subgroup analysis were shown in Additional file 6.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies the groups used in syntheses (age, variant, country, study population, study type) and links these subgroup analyses to the synthesis results and tables/forest plots."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Therefore, the aim of this study was to conduct a meta-analysis to describe and summarize the current reinfection rates in different regions and among diverse populations. Furthermore, the study sought to examine trends in reinfection rates over time, so as to provide reference for the development of global unified reinfection standards and reinfection measures in the future. reinfection working definition in this study was based on a positive laboratory result at least 90 days after laboratory confirmation of primary infection(...), advised from WHO and US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention [21, 22].",
      "reason": "The manuscript provides rationale for notable eligibility choices: the 90-day reinfection working definition is justified by WHO/CDC guidance and the need to reduce heterogeneity over time. Additionally, exclusions such as grey literature are discussed as limitations elsewhere (see text_19), supporting rationale for that restriction.",
      "confidence": 85
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "85"
}

